\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 So, here's the numbers. July, typically the hottest month of the year across most of the United States. This one, 3.3 degrees hotter than the 20th century average. So, July 2026 will go down, the average temperature, 76.9 degrees Fahrenheit. Again, a fever of 3.3 degrees will make you really sick. This earth fever, as we've got, is shattering records. More than 600 heat records. That's both for maximum and minimum. Overnight, hot temperatures were set. Only less than 50, fewer than 50 cold records. And on a planet in balance, those numbers should even out most years.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Top five warmest Julys for 13 states that we know of right now. And of course, the cause of all of this is climate change due to fossil fuel pollution blanketing the earth right now. We have a series of these heat domes, which is directly related to that. As the jet stream breaks down, you get these lobes of heat that come down. And then, of course, we've got the intensifying super El Nino in the Pacific, John, which is kind of like having a fever and then going into a sauna. You know, that's a natural phenomenon. But earth is already so overheated based on and we're at just about 1.5 degrees of global warming. You know, where the countries agreed, this is the limit to stop. But this could be one of the coolest Julys now the rest of our lives.}
